The expression for the change of entropy between any two states is found by dividing either of the expressions for <math>dH</math> in (8) by <math>\theta</math> and integrating between the given limits, since <math>dH/\theta</math> is a perfect differential. In the case of a solid or a liquid, the latent heat of isothermal expansion may often be neglected, and if the specific heat, <math>s</math>, be also taken as constant, we have simply <math>\phi - \phi_0 = s \log_e\theta/\theta_0</math>. If the substance at the temperature <math>\theta</math> undergoes a change of state absorbing latent heat, <math>L</math>, we have merely to add the term <math>L/\theta</math> to the above expression. In the case of an ideal gas, <math>dp/d\theta</math> at constant volume <math>= R/v</math>, and <math>dv/d\theta</math> at constant pressure <math>= R/p</math>; thus we obtain the expressions for the change of entropy <math>\phi - \phi_0</math> from the state <math>p_0\theta_0v_0</math> to the state <math>p\theta v</math>,
{{block center|<math>\phi - \phi_0 = s \log_e\theta/\theta_0 + R \log_e v/v_0</math>}}
{{block center|<math>= S \log_e\theta/\theta_0 - R \log_e p/p_0</math> . . . (32)}}
In the case of an imperfect gas or vapour, the above expressions are frequently employed, but a more accurate result may be obtained by employing equation (17) with the value of the specific heat, <math>S</math>, from (29), which gives the expression
{{block center|<math>\phi - \phi_0 = S_0 \log_e\theta/\theta_0 - R \log_e p/p_0 - n(cp/\theta - c_0p_0/\theta_0)</math> . . (33)}}
